 ### [Locality-preserving Densification](9585-locality-preserving-densification.md)
-Locality-preserving densification addresses the memory layout bottleneck in 3D Gaussian Splatting training, where the addition of new Gaussians to the end of parameter buffers causes spatially close primitives to be stored far apart in memory. This spatial fragmentation leads to uncoalesced memory accesses and increased warp divergence during rasterization. By reordering Gaussians based on their 3D spatial proximity using Z-ordering (Morton order), the method ensures that neighboring Gaussians in space are also adjacent in memory, thereby improving cache efficiency and reducing training time for scenes with high Gaussian counts. It is distinct from score-based or group-based densification strategies by focusing exclusively on the memory layout optimization rather than the geometric distribution or selection criteria of the primitives.
