 ### [Progressive and Multi-stage Training](9966-progressive-and-multi-stage-training.md)
-Progressive and Multi-stage Training structures the optimization of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) into discrete temporal phases to improve rendering quality and training stability. It solves the problem of instability caused by dense initialization and the inefficiency of uniform growth by transitioning from coarse global structure learning to fine-grained local refinement. Unlike single-stage training, which treats all primitives equally from the start, this approach employs specific densification heuristics, loss functions, or supervision signals tailored to each stage.
+[Progressive and Multi-stage Training](9966-progressive-and-multi-stage-training.md) refers to methodologies that divide the optimization of 3D Gaussian representations into distinct phases or stages, rather than training the primitive set in a single uniform pass. It solves the instability, poor convergence, and structural incompleteness inherent in single-stage optimization by first establishing a global structural approximation or coarse geometry, and then progressively refining local details through densification or fine-tuning. This approach yields significantly higher rendering quality compared to single-stage baselines, as evidenced by improved PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS metrics on standard benchmarks.
 
 ### [Gaussian Insertion and Pruning](10074-gaussian-insertion-and-pruning.md)
-Gaussian Insertion and Pruning is a densification strategy designed for monocular and RGB-D SLAM systems using 3D Gaussian Splatting, specifically addressing the initialization of new primitives in unseen regions and the removal of geometrically unstable outliers. Unlike standard offline densification which relies on gradient norms or screen-space error, this method uses visibility consistency across a sliding window of keyframes to validate new Gaussians. It is distinct from [Adaptive Density Control](9188-adaptive-density-control.md) by prioritizing SLAM stability over reconstruction fidelity, and from [Score-based Densification](4705-score-based-densification.md) by using explicit depth sampling and cross-view visibility checks rather than accumulated loss gradients.
