 ### [Texel Size Adaptation and Primitive Management](12592-texel-size-adaptation-and-primitive-management.md)
-Texel Size Adaptation and Primitive Management is a content-aware optimization routine that dynamically adjusts the resolution of per-primitive texture maps and the number of 3D Gaussian primitives during training. It solves the problem of balancing model capacity against appearance quality by allocating texels and primitives only where necessary, preventing overfitting and memory exhaustion. Unlike standard densification strategies that rely primarily on gradient norms or screen-space area, this method uses a quantile-based error threshold to drive both primitive splitting and texture upscaling/downscaling, allowing the model to automatically adjust the ratio of primitives to texels based on scene complexity.
