 ## Background
-The concept of adaptive density control was introduced with the original 3D Gaussian Splatting paper by Kerbl et al. (2023), which established the baseline algorithm for splitting and cloning Gaussians based on 2D gradient magnitudes and screen-space size [S8, unofficial]. Subsequent research has expanded this family to address specific limitations, such as the instability of pruning [4705-score-based-densification.md], the computational cost of dynamic scenes [6351-groupflow.md], and the need for efficient memory layout [9585-locality-preserving-densification.md]. The strategy is now a standard component of 3DGS training pipelines, with numerous variants tailored for SLAM [10074-gaussian-insertion-and-pruning.md], compression [12354-pruning-and-sparsification.md], and generative modeling [11473-hybrid-and-diffusion-representations.md].
+The concept of adaptive density control was introduced with the original 3D Gaussian Splatting paper by Kerbl et al. (2023), which established the baseline algorithm for splitting and cloning Gaussians based on 2D gradient magnitudes [S1, unofficial][S8, unofficial]. Since then, numerous variants have been developed to address specific limitations such as memory efficiency [S4], dynamic scene modeling [S5, unofficial], sparse-view reconstruction [9132-vs-splat-voxel-selection.md], and training stability [S3]. The field has evolved from simple heuristic-based splitting to more sophisticated methods involving MCMC sampling [7852-3dgs-mcmc.md], score-based selection [4705-score-based-densification.md], and generative priors [11473-hybrid-and-diffusion-representations.md].
 
